THE DARK HOURS by Michael Connelly

2021. Allen&Unwin. Paperback.

This is my first Connelly after "The Law of Innocence" and is
coincidentally the next Connelly book. I've got about five recent
Connelly's in a pile; Nicole Ruddy gives me one a year after she's
finished reading them; which works just fine for me. I'm about four
recent Connelly's behind but they're fast and entertaining reads and
by the time I get the absolute newest from Nicole (I'm guessing for
Christmas) I'll probably be caught up.

This is my first Renee Ballard read; she's a good character. I think
I read the first twenty pages of "The Late Show" years ago but put
it aside for some reason. I'll get back to that book soon enough. The
background of true events in The Dark Hours is the increasing COVID
restrictions and the January 6 Washington riots. While I've been
reading The Dark Hours,  COVID Doctor Anthony Fauci is being put
through the wringer in public enquiries and has pleaded the fifth
against self-incrimination over 110 times.

There's some nice humour in this book (dark hours humour?) in
particular Ballard's useless and frequently absent offsider Detective
Moore is a figure of fun. There's a nosy neighbour character who
annoys Ballard and the reader but who turns out to have no involvement
in the rapes whatsoever; just as the reader suspects he'll be part of
the climax.

There are two cases for the price of one in this book; money-lending
dentists killing their clients once they can no longer afford the
repayments, and a creepy rape-for-hire duo who misogynistic men can
hire on the Dark Web to punish ex-partners. The last few pages of the
book were very satisfying as Ballard and Bosch round up one of the
customers who hired the rapists, and plan the shutdown of the
operation with the help of the FBI.

